<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Force East Timor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1999">1999</a>–<a href="/years/2000">2000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as INTERFET</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Force <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a> (INTERFET) was a multinational peacekeeping force led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and authorised by the UN Security Council in September 1999. It deployed after pro-Indonesian militias devastated East Timor following its vote for independence, restored security and oversaw the departure of Indonesian forces. In February 2000 it handed over to the UN Transitional Administration in East Timor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-force-east-timor.jpg" alt="International Force East Timor" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unbekannt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:INTERFET_Logo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> invaded and annexed the former Portuguese colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a> in 1975, an annexation the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> never recognised and only one state, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, recognised, in 1979. After the fall of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> his successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-j-habibie">B. J. Habibie</a>, was prepared to grant East Timor special autonomy. A letter from the Australian prime minister John Howard in late 1998, suggesting a plebiscite after a long period of autonomy, upset Habibie, who announced a snap referendum instead. Pro-Indonesian militias reacted violently, and the Indonesian army did not intervene.</p><p>The referendum of 30 August 1999 showed overwhelming support for independence. After the result was announced on 4 September, violence broke out in which many East Timorese were killed and as many as 500,000 displaced. Under international pressure Habibie agreed on 12 September to accept a peacekeeping force, and on 15 September the Security Council passed Resolution 1264, calling for a multinational force to restore peace and security, protect the UN mission and support humanitarian work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The force began deploying on 20 September 1999 under Major General Peter Cosgrove. Australian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and British special forces secured Komoro Airport at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dili">Dili</a>, and infantry followed. Cosgrove adopted an oil spot approach, dominating key areas and moving quickly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> to keep the militias off balance. On 6 October troops entered Suai, capturing 116 militia, and on 16 October an Australian patrol fought off repeated attacks at Aidabasalala. Indonesia formally recognised the referendum result on 19 October, and on 22 October the force began securing the Oecussi Enclave, the last part of the country.</p><p>Twenty-two nations eventually contributed, and the force grew to more than 11,500. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> provided 5,500 personnel at its peak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> 1,200, its largest overseas deployment since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, prevented by law from sending troops, gave US$100 million. On 28 February 2000 INTERFET handed command of military operations to the UN Transitional Administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>No serious incident occurred between Indonesian and coalition forces, but relations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> took several years to recover. The operation also strained Australia's military logistics, which had not had to supply a deployed force in full since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and had been run down by a decade of reduced defence spending; whether they could have sustained a more intense deployment was questionable.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “International Force East Timor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The vote of August 1999 brought a last campaign of destruction by pro-integration militias supported by elements of the Indonesian military, and the multinational force <b>INTERFET</b> was allowed in to restore order.</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · Military</a></li></ul></section>
